It will:

1. Convert your current `packageInfo` object to a JSON string (so the helper can work with it).  
2. Tell the helper to set the property `"version"` to the string `"1.2.3"`.  
3. Parse the returned JSON string back into a JavaScript object.  

If `packageInfo.version` already equals `"1.2.3"` (or the property doesn’t exist), the helper will simply return the original object unchanged.

```js
// Assume you already have a `packageInfo` object somewhere in your code
// e.g. const packageInfo = { name: "my-lib", version: "1.0.0" };

// 1. Call the helper
const updatedJson = functions.vOe({
  r: JSON.stringify(packageInfo),   // the whole object as a string
  e: "version",                    // property to update
  t: "\"1.2.3\""                   // new value, also as a string
});

// 2. Convert the returned JSON string back to an object
const updatedPackageInfo = JSON.parse(updatedJson);

// 3. (Optional) Replace the old object if you want to keep the same reference
Object.assign(packageInfo, updatedPackageInfo);

// Now `packageInfo.version` is guaranteed to be "1.2.3" (unless it was already that value)
console.log(updatedPackageInfo);
```

### What the helper does

- **`r`** – the JSON string of the object you want to modify.  
- **`e`** – the property name (`"version"`).  
- **`t`** – the new value, also JSON‑encoded (`"\"1.2.3\""`).

The helper checks whether the property exists and whether its current value differs from the new value. If either condition is true, it updates the property; otherwise, it leaves the object untouched.

